{"images":[],"manifest":{"budgetTokens":60000,"countMethod":"proxy","model":"gpt-6-sol","packSha":"465053d61a555c213b4d2976c16d4883b8c4ea0ae54c4d407918c61e16f9c09d","packTokens":8521,"recipeVersion":"1","redactions":0,"sections":[{"id":"platform","tokens":215,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"tpl","tokens":1850,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"taste","tokens":1054,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"field-ideas","tokens":428,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"reqs","tokens":4941,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"task","tokens":16,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"}],"stage":"design"},"pointers":[],"system":"PLATFORM. This product is designed and built as a web app, and nothing else. Where the requirements ask for a mobile app, a phone app, an iOS or Android app, treat them as the same features in one responsive web app that works well on a phone's browser: never stop, park, ask or leave a requirement out because it names a mobile app, and never plan a native app, an app store step or a phone-only frame. Every page is responsive: it is laid out for a phone, a tablet and a desktop width, with touch-sized controls on the narrow one. What only a native app can do (push notifications, background location, a home-screen icon) is given its closest web form (an in-app notice or email, the browser's location prompt, an installable web app) and said so.\n\nYou list the screens of a product for Google Stitch to draw, one prompt per screen.\n- One screen per page a user sees: ids S-1, S-2, ..., each with its route, a proposed file path and the requirement ids it serves in \"reqs\".\n- Every requirement is on a screen, or in \"noScreen\" with the reason it has no page of its own.\n- Each \"prompt\" describes that page alone, in its normal state (its other states are drawn apart, from \"states\"): its purpose, its sections in order, and real sample content from the product's domain (names, amounts, dates). No lorem ipsum, no generic names, nothing the requirements do not ask for.\n- \"links\" lists every way from this page to another screen: its menu items and the buttons and links that open one, each with the exact words on the control (\"label\") and the screen id it opens (\"to\"). A screen has one label, the same on every page that links to it; a button that stays on its page is not listed. The prompt uses those same words for those controls. The pages one kind of user sees share one menu: the same items, labels and order on each of them, and never a page meant for another kind of user.\n- Where a page shows something its user knows by sight (a product, a place, a person, a vehicle), the prompt asks for a real photograph of it, with what is in the picture; never an illustration, an icon in its place or a grey box.\n- \"field-ideas\", when given, tells how the leading real products of this field arrange their pages. Where a requirement fits one of their patterns, the prompt asks for that component by what it is and what it holds (a boarding-pass strip with the route set large, a balance in large tabular figures above the latest movements), with this product's own content. Never a product's name, logo or wording, and never a colour from that list: the design system sets the colours.\n- \"client-references\", when given, are the client's own references with their pictures (R-1, R-2, ...). Stitch never sees them, so the prompt says in words what is taken: for a \"layout\" or \"match\" reference, the navigation and the regions in the order its picture shows, on the screens it relates to; for an \"inspire\" reference, its feel (how dense, how much white, how large the pictures and headings). The requirements beat a reference.\n- \"shells\" lists the frame each kind of user's pages share, one per kind (ids such as \"employee\", \"admin\"). Its \"header\" says in two or three sentences what the header holds, the same on every page: the brand's words as written, where the menu sits (beside the brand or centred), and what is on the right (the signed-in user's name and role, say; whether their picture is a photograph or initials, and on which side of the name it sits), and the brand's mark in one phrase (a small square in the accent with a white \"P\", or the words alone). Each screen names its shell in \"shell\"; a page outside any (sign-in) leaves it out. Every prompt of a shell is given that header word for word, so a prompt never describes the header itself.\n- \"sample\" is the product's sample data, the same on every page: each person with their name, department and role, what the pages show of them (their space, their vehicle and its plate), the place's one time zone, and the counts more than one page shows (capacity per floor, totals), all agreeing with each other, and, when any page lists events (a log, a history, a report, a check), one dated list of them (when, who, what, where, the result) that those pages all take their rows from, with counts that add up to it. Every prompt takes its names, figures and dates from it and never contradicts it; one person is never two people's photograph.\n- Labels, headings and messages use the plain words a user of this product would say (\"Give up my space today\", \"3 spaces free\"), never system or legal wording (\"relinquish\", \"allocation governance\", \"materialize\"). Each page has one primary action; one that cannot be undone says so beside its button.\n- \"title\" is the page's one heading. A page the menu opens has its menu label as its title, word for word; a menu label names a place (\"My space\", \"Free spaces\"), never an action, in three words at most.\n- A page never repeats its menu as a panel of links or tiles. A row that opens a record is itself the link, or has one control that does; no checkbox or radio on a row unless the page acts on several rows at once. Filters apply as they are chosen, with no Apply button. A page shows no field, id, switch, building, gate or device that neither the requirements nor \"sample\" name.\n- \"phone\": true on the pages a person opens on a phone on the move (the main tasks of the largest group of users), five at most; each is drawn a second time at phone width.\n- Each prompt composes its page like a finished product, not a wireframe: one thing the eye meets first (named, with its size against the rest), then the sections in the order the user needs them, what sits beside what on a wide screen, and where the page is left empty on purpose. Give real figures, names and dates enough to fill every list and table with believable rows.\n- \"taste\" holds the layout rules, the anti-patterns and the component replacements the design system is written by. Compose each page by them: where a page would have a component from the left column of its table, the prompt asks for the one on the right, with this product's content (one headline figure with its context above a dense table, never four cards of figures with sparklines; a list or a table, never a grid of near-equal tiles; no row of three equal cards). Its lines about colours, fonts, shadows, motion and CSS are the design system's and stay out of a prompt, and its lines about heroes, pricing, testimonials and footers apply to a public marketing page alone. A page outside any shell that holds one task (sign in, a confirmation, a message that access is refused) is not a hero: its heading, its one sentence and its one action sit in a single panel no wider than 440 px, centred on the page both ways, with the brand's mark and words above the panel and nothing else on the page (no header bar, no footer, no office or place name). The requirements, and a rule above, win over it.\n- \"states\" lists the page's extra states besides its normal page (empty, loading, error, success, validation) only where the requirements need them.\n- \"brand\" lists the client's colours (hex) and fonts only when the requirements or references name them; otherwise leave it empty.\n\n### 6. Define Layout Principles\n- No overlapping elements — every element occupies its own clear spatial zone. No absolute-positioned content stacking\n- Centered Hero sections are BANNED when variance exceeds 4 — force Split Screen, Left-Aligned, or Asymmetric Whitespace\n- The generic \"3 equal cards horizontally\" feature row is BANNED — use 2-column Zig-Zag, asymmetric grid, or horizontal scroll\n- CSS Grid over Flexbox math — never use `calc()` percentage hacks\n- Contain layouts using max-width constraints (e.g., 1400px centered)\n- Full-height sections must use `min-h-[100dvh]` — never `h-screen` (iOS Safari catastrophic jump)\n\n### 9. List Anti-Patterns (AI Tells)\nEncode these as explicit \"NEVER DO\" rules in the DESIGN.md:\n- No emojis anywhere\n- No `Inter` font\n- No generic serif fonts (`Times New Roman`, `Georgia`, `Garamond`) — distinctive modern serifs only if needed\n- No pure black (`#000000`)\n- No neon/outer glow shadows\n- No oversaturated accents\n- No excessive gradient text on large headers\n- No custom mouse cursors\n- No overlapping elements — clean spatial separation always\n- No 3-column equal card layouts\n- No generic names (\"John Doe\", \"Acme\", \"Nexus\")\n- No fake round numbers (`99.99%`, `50%`)\n- No AI copywriting clichés (\"Elevate\", \"Seamless\", \"Unleash\", \"Next-Gen\")\n- No filler UI text: \"Scroll to explore\", \"Swipe down\", scroll arrows, bouncing chevrons\n- No broken Unsplash links — use `picsum.photos` or SVG avatars\n- No centered Hero sections (for high-variance projects)\n\n## 12. Anti-Vibe-Coded Components (extends §5 and §9)\n\nThese are the components that make an app look generated. For each, the DESIGN.md must specify the substitute.\n\n| AI-default component | Replace with |\n|---|---|\n| Pill badge above hero (\"New: AI-powered ✨\") | Nothing, or one dated plain-text line in mono (e.g. \"v2.4 — 3 Oct\") |\n| Feature cards: icon in rounded square + title + 2 lines | Numbered editorial list with real product crops, or annotated screenshot callouts |\n| \"Trusted by\" grayscale logo strip | One specific quote with full name, role and context — or omit |\n| Stats row \"10K+ / 99.9% / 24/7\" | One real metric with its source and timeframe |\n| 3-tier pricing with highlighted \"Most Popular\" middle | Plan comparison table with honest limits, or a single plan with usage-based detail |\n| Testimonial carousel with stars and circular avatars | 1–2 long quotes, set typographically large, no stars |\n| Gradient mesh / blob / glow backgrounds | Flat surface; structure through hairline rules, type scale and spacing |\n| Glassmorphism cards (`backdrop-blur` + white/10) | Solid surfaces with a defined border or a tint step |\n| Bento grid of near-equal tiles | Only when content genuinely differs in size; otherwise a list or table |\n| Dashboard: 4 KPI cards each with sparkline and green \"+12%\" | One headline metric with context, then a dense, sortable table |\n| Hero split: headline left, floating phone/3D render right | The product's actual UI, cropped tight to the interesting part |\n| Sparkle/wand icon for anything \"AI\" | A label that says what the feature does |\n| \"Get started\" / \"Start for free\" CTA | A product-specific verb (\"Plan your first route\", \"Import a spreadsheet\") |\n| Default shadcn look (zinc, 0.5rem radius, ring) on everything | A deliberate radius system (see §13) and a custom focus style |\n| Icon on every list item and nav link | Icons only where they speed up recognition; text labels carry meaning |\n| Mega footer with 4 link columns + newsletter box | Compact footer sized to the real number of links |\n| Uniform section template repeated down the page | Vary section rhythm: full-bleed, narrow text column, dense table, quote |","tools":[],"user":"How the leading products of this field arrange their pages:\nRetail and marketplaces:\n- Amazon: plain bar, soft corners; dark navy bar, orange action, very dense results\n- Walmart: filled bar, round corners; blue bar, yellow spark, big price text\n- Target: plain bar, round corners; red on white, clean grid of images\n- IKEA: plain bar, sharp corners; blue and yellow on white, plain product grid\n- Etsy: plain bar, round corners; warm orange with serif touches, image-led\n- Zara: plain bar, sharp corners; black and white only, huge photography, tiny type\nWhat their pages share: White pages, big product imagery and one warm high-energy action colour (orange, red or blue) on the buy button. Price is the heaviest text. Marketplaces are dense with filters; fashion brands go minimal in black and white. Choose where on that range the product sits.\n\nRide-hailing and mobility:\n- Uber: plain bar, soft corners; pure black and white, no accent colour, map-led\n- Lyft: plain bar, round corners; hot pink on white or deep purple, friendly\n- Bolt: plain bar, round corners; fresh green on white\n- Grab: filled bar, round corners; green bar, white cards, super-app grid\n- Careem: plain bar, round corners; green with warm greys\nWhat their pages share: The map is the page; the brand colour appears on the one primary action and on the selected vehicle or route. Surfaces are white or black with big touch targets and a bottom sheet for details. Trip status is a short timeline (arriving, on trip, done).\n\n<artifact id=\"reqs\" kind=\"requirements\" sha=\"\">\n[\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-1\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian responsive web app shall provide role-oriented portal pages and navigation for stock, orders, deliveries, temperature monitoring, quality decisions, and recalls on phone, tablet, and desktop browser widths.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-2\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian responsive web app shall provide staff authentication through Meridian Microsoft accounts and customer authentication using email and password followed by a one-time code, with reset links requested by email and no social sign-in or single-sign-on option for customer accounts.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-3\",\n  \"ears\": \"When a user opens portal views, the Meridian responsive web app shall show records and actions returned for that user's role, assigned warehouses, and customer organisation, present Auditor views as read-only, and clear protected data when the API refuses access.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-4\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian responsive web app shall let a Customer Administrator invite and remove users through controls scoped to that administrator's customer organisation.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-5\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian responsive web app shall let System Administrators list, search, view, deactivate, and maintain products and create versioned temperature profiles with storage bands, contiguous non-overlapping tiers, allowance or not-permitted designations, a reason, and an effective date no earlier than today, while keeping earlier versions read-only.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-6\",\n  \"ears\": \"When a temperature-profile version takes effect, the Meridian responsive web app shall show the new version and API-returned recalculated budgets, decisions, and current statuses for stock still held from its effective date onward, show earlier quality decisions as unchanged, and show resulting order, box, and route statuses for affected allocated or packed stock.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-7\",\n  \"ears\": \"When a Warehouse Operator records a receipt, the Meridian responsive web app shall capture product, lot number, expiry date, quantity, supplier, and storage location and show returned quarantine status and inbound temperature information before a release decision.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-8\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian responsive web app shall provide inbound logger-file upload and display returned temperature history and budget use, validate separate image or PDF receipt-evidence files, and accept logger exports in the supplier's documented format.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-9\",\n  \"ears\": \"When quality staff review a receipt or excursion, the Meridian responsive web app shall present available release or disposition actions and show decision states returned by the API, including separate proposal and approval steps for not-permitted-range dispositions.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-10\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian responsive web app shall show on-hand stock with mutually exclusive quantities for available, allocated, quarantined, On Hold, Rejected, Recalled, and Expired statuses, and show returned warehouse, lot, product, receipt, and location details.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-11\",\n  \"ears\": \"When portions of received stock have different temperature histories, the Meridian responsive web app shall show them as separate handling units with their returned quantity, history, and tier-budget use and remaining.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-12\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian responsive web app shall provide stock-movement forms and show the returned movement record with source and destination locations, quantity, actor, and time.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-13\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian responsive web app shall let Customer Administrators maintain their organisation's saved delivery addresses and let System Administrators set the organisation's minimum remaining shelf-life requirement, showing that value read-only to customer users and Dispatchers.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-14\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian responsive web app shall let customer users create orders with one or more product lines, pack quantities, an organisation-saved delivery address, an eligible delivery date, and a choice between delivery of available quantities and complete delivery only.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-15\",\n  \"ears\": \"When customers view, change, or cancel an order, the Meridian responsive web app shall show order status and allocated and outstanding quantities per line, expose customer change and cancellation controls only before Packed, expose dispatcher cancellation controls when returned as available, and display API outcomes.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-16\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian responsive web app shall show order allocations by line and lot, including returned expiry dates and quantities, expose dispatcher lot-override controls with a required reason and API outcome, and show returned allocation and stock-status outcomes in order, box, and route views.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-17\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian responsive web app shall let Warehouse Operators pack stock into shipper boxes by type, associate one logger with each box, and show compatibility, contents, and sealing details returned by the API.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-18\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian responsive web app shall let Dispatchers create and view routes with a date, vehicle, driver, ordered stops, and planned arrival times and show route-confirmation errors and confirmed-route changes returned by the API, including configured qualified-duration safety margins.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-19\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian responsive web app shall show temperature histories for boxes, cold rooms, and lots as charts and tables with returned readings, applicable bands, excursions, gaps, and reading flags or silence status.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-20\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian responsive web app shall show excursions, handling-unit budget use and remaining allowances, returned alerts, and returned hold and decision statuses for affected devices and stock.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-21\",\n  \"ears\": \"When the API returns an unresolved temperature-history gap affecting stock, the Meridian responsive web app shall show the gap, review status, and stock status returned by the API without inferring an On Hold status from the gap or displaying an estimated temperature tier.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-22\",\n  \"ears\": \"When the API returns an unresolved temperature-history gap affecting stock, the Meridian responsive web app shall show the returned stock status and budget values without presenting an estimated charge to an unobserved temperature tier.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-23\",\n  \"ears\": \"When corrected temperature history identifies an excursion affecting stock already delivered, the Meridian responsive web app shall show the returned quality-review case to the relevant customer and staff users.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-24\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian responsive web app shall provide an installable, responsive driver experience that caches the current workday's assigned route, queues supported loading and delivery actions for synchronization when connectivity returns, retains unsynchronized actions tied to the originating driver across sign-out and workday end until synchronization succeeds or the API rejects or refuses them, synchronizes those actions only after that same driver signs in again, clears cached route and delivery data when the driver signs out, the workday ends, or access is refused, erases or marks queued actions rejected when the API refuses access or reports the account disabled, and shows stale-route and queued-action outcomes returned by the API.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-25\",\n  \"ears\": \"When a driver records a delivery handover, the Meridian responsive web app shall capture the recipient's name, explicit electronic acknowledgment, quantities handed over, and any refusal or partial-delivery reason, and show outstanding quantities and returned delivery status, including restrictions returned when an offline handover synchronizes.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-26\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian responsive web app shall let Quality Managers start recalls by selecting affected lots and providing a reason, view and manage recall records, and close recalls, and let customer users submit a quantity and stated disposition for each affected delivery, showing returned reconciliation and stock-block information.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-27\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian responsive web app shall provide filtered on-screen operational lists and CSV export, and show creator and last-changer identity and timestamps as read-only metadata where returned.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-28\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian responsive web app shall provide English and Arabic portal content and render Arabic pages right to left.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-29\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian responsive web app shall provide individual email-and-password sign-in and self-service password reset for driver, staff, and customer areas, including a form to set a new password from a reset link and a way to request another reset email when the link is invalid or expired.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-30\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall provide System Administrator screens to create and deactivate accounts and assign roles, staff warehouse access, and customer-organisation access.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-31\",\n  \"ears\": \"When a driver opens today's routes, the Meridian web app shall show only that driver's assigned routes and each route's stops in route order.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-32\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall compare scanned shipper boxes with expected boxes before route departure, report missing or wrong-route boxes, and keep departure blocked until all expected boxes are scanned and wrong boxes are removed or corrected.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-33\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall show dispatchers each route stop's status and last-updated time.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-34\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall show each route box's latest available temperature, status, and alerts from the supplied gateway data and identify cached temperature data as stale while disconnected.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-35\",\n  \"ears\": \"When scanned stock is On Hold, the Meridian web app shall display “Do not deliver” and the returned hold reason and prevent handover of that box.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-36\",\n  \"ears\": \"When the driver is online and the service reports a scanned box as Recalled, the Meridian web app shall display “Do not deliver” and prevent handover of that box.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-37\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall let drivers record an eligible handover with receiver name, signature, delivery photo, actual handover time, and available browser location.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-38\",\n  \"ears\": \"If browser location is denied or unavailable, then the Meridian web app shall allow an otherwise eligible handover and record location as unavailable.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-39\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall let drivers record full or partial refusal only by whole box and require a reason for each refused box.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-40\",\n  \"ears\": \"While the browser is offline, the Meridian web app shall retain loading, handover, and refusal work locally across session expiry or sign-out, allow a driver to record that work after session expiry, require the driver to re-authenticate before queued work syncs, automatically send queued work when connectivity returns, preserve handover occurrence time and identifier on retries, clear queued payload only after successful service acknowledgement, and show an error without reporting work as queued when local storage cannot retain the complete payload.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-41\",\n  \"ears\": \"When an assigned route changes while a driver is offline, the Meridian web app shall show route differences, require review and resolution of affected unsent work before continuing, and preserve completed offline handovers.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-42\",\n  \"ears\": \"When an offline handover conflicts with an On Hold status upon synchronization, the Meridian web app shall retain and flag the handover and display the returned quality-case result.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-43\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall show Quality reviewers the full excursion temperature history, allowance used and remaining for each tier, and the returned recommendation, using the configured tier rules.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-44\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall present a single-person quality-decision flow only when an excursion is within the first tier above or below the permitted band, allowance remains, and the proposed decision matches the recommendation, and otherwise present a Quality Officer proposal and separate approval by a different Quality Manager; a written justification is required when a decision opposes the recommendation.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-45\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall require identity confirmation using the account's sign-in method and a reason when a user signs a quality decision, and present signed decisions as read-only with a separate superseding-decision action.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-46\",\n  \"ears\": \"When the service returns a quality disposition, the Meridian web app shall show resulting stock, order, and route status and the earlier and current disposition when superseded.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-47\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall collect destruction date, quantity, and witness for rejected stock and prevent entry of quantity greater than the displayed rejected quantity.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-48\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall identify quality decisions waiting more than four hours as escalated in the Quality Manager's waiting-decisions view.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-49\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall provide a Quality Manager flow to start and close recalls with signed actions, collecting product, one or more lots, reason, and severity for recall start, and display each returned action result.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-50\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall display the returned recall trace, including warehouse locations, boxes, routes, customer deliveries, quantities, delivery dates, receivers, and the trace total alongside received quantity when supplied.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-51\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall show customer recall notices in the portal, link notices to affected deliveries, and provide a response entry point.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-52\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall let customers submit or update per-delivery on-hand, used or dispensed, and destroyed quantities, allow a total below the delivered amount as unaccounted, and reject a total above it.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-53\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall show recall progress as the returned percentage accounted for, identify outstanding customers and reminder status when supplied, and let a Quality Manager submit a signed write-off justification for unaccounted quantity.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-54\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall show customers only their organisation's current and past orders, allocated lots and expiry dates, order status, and in-transit delivery arrival and temperature status, without exposing other route customers.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-55\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall provide a PDF certificate for each completed delivery in the signed-in customer's organisation with products, lots, expiry dates, quantities, receiver, handover time, each box's temperature chart from sealing to handover, excursions, and signed excursion decisions.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-56\",\n  \"ears\": \"When late readings produce a new certificate version, the Meridian web app shall list the new and earlier versions for the signed-in customer's completed delivery and keep each available for download.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-57\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall present the customer portal and certificates in the user's selected English or Arabic, render Arabic right to left including tables and charts, and show dates and times in the account's configured time zone.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-58\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall provide an audit-trail screen showing returned entries with actor, time, changed fields, old and new values, and supplied reason, with filters by record, user, date range, and action type and export of the filtered view.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-59\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall show the returned history of a lot from receipt through delivery or destruction in time order.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-60\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall provide on-screen reports for selected date ranges and warehouses, with CSV export of displayed figures, including excursion counts and rates by route, driver, vehicle, and shipper box type; packs rejected and destroyed by product and cause; deliveries on time within 60 minutes of planned arrival and in full when every ordered pack is handed over, each as a percentage; average time from excursion to decision; stock within 60 days of expiry by product and warehouse; and open recalls and their progress.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-61\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall provide forms to enter opening lots and stock records and submit them to the service.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-62\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall make recorded handover signatures and delivery photos available from a delivery record within its seven-year evidence-retention period.\"\n }\n]\n</artifact>\n\nList every screen and write one Stitch prompt for each."}